{
  "_doc": "Knowledge pacing of ruleset 3.0 (design chapter 36, section 2.7). Read by crates/bts-engine/src/content.rs at load; no technology id is paced in code. 'tiers' gives, per graph tier, the evidence at which a node becomes a lead (hint; discovered at 2.5 x hint), the research work in thousands of researcher-months and the minimum months of an investigation. 'eras' are pacing classes for nodes whose graph tier misplaces them in history: each has its own hint threshold and the median-seat target window (historical years, negative = BC) its threshold was calibrated for. 'nodes' assigns a node to an era and may also give the tier and evidence tags of a node the code does not know (nodes added to content/technologies.json later); a node absent here is paced by its tier.",
  "map_scale": {
    "_doc": "Larger maps hold larger civilizations with more practitioners and more research posts. Evidence thresholds (technologies and practices) and research and reform work are multiplied by 1 + bp x (regions - reference) / reference / 10,000: work with the expected research capacity (about +25% on 60 regions), evidence with practitioner activity, which grows as the square root of the people engaged (about half as fast).",
